    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "title": "Why stopping asthma medicine when you feel fine can be risky",
      "excerpt": "Why keep taking medicine when you have no symptoms at all? Doctors used to think the same thing. Here's how asthma treatment has changed over the past few decades.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "A conversation like this comes up often in the exam room."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"Doctor, I haven't been coughing at all lately, so I've been skipping my inhaler!\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "It's wonderful to hear that symptoms have settled down, and I understand how tiring it can be to keep up a daily medication. But this is actually the biggest trap in asthma treatment."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll understand why you need to keep taking medicine even with no symptoms at all, so you can keep up treatment without that nagging doubt. The short version: **even on symptom-free days, inflammation keeps quietly smoldering in your airways, so an inhaler isn't a \"cure\" — it's what keeps the embers from reigniting.** Here's why, step by step."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Even on symptom-free days, the airways are quietly smoldering"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Until around the 1980s, asthma was thought of as a condition where the airways only tighten up during an attack, so treatment mainly focused on medicines that calmed an attack once it started. Later research, however, showed that **allergic inflammation keeps smoldering in the airway lining even on calm days with no symptoms at all.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If a severe attack is a \"big fire,\" a symptom-free day is more like a \"fire smoldering without smoke.\" It may look calm on the surface, but a small trigger — dust, cold air — can set it ablaze again at any time."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Two reasons stopping medicine makes a flare-up more likely"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Since this discovery, asthma treatment has shifted dramatically — from simply \"stopping an attack\" to preventive treatment (such as inhaled steroids) aimed at **putting out the allergic embers every single day.** There are two main reasons to keep taking it daily even when you feel fine."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- **You're not \"cured\" — the medicine is simply holding things down.** Your inhaler is suppressing the embers, which is why you have no symptoms. Stop, and they can start smoldering again.\n- **Left unchecked, the airways can stiffen (a process called remodeling).** Repeated inflammation can thicken and harden the airway walls over time, potentially progressing to a stubborn form of asthma that responds less well to medication down the road."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### So is there ever a day you can cut back?"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Of course, it's not a treatment you continue at the same dose forever. But that decision shouldn't be based on \"I've felt fine lately\" — it's something your doctor judges by combining several measures, such as **whether symptoms have been stable over several months and whether you're waking at night from coughing or breathlessness**, together with a **FeNO (fractional exhaled nitric oxide) test**, which we plan to offer at our clinic once we open. With just a single breath out, it gives an on-the-spot, objective number for how much allergic inflammation is still smoldering in your airways — no blood draw or bulky equipment needed. Once those signs line up, we may consider a step-down, gradually reducing the inhaled dose. Conversely, during pollen or dust season, or when you catch a cold, we may temporarily increase it — this isn't a treatment where the dose stays fixed for life either."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "In other words, what matters is not stopping or reducing on your own judgment, but **checking in with us at each milestone so we can work out together what dose is right for you right now.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Let's protect airways that don't flare up — together"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Taking a daily inhaler or medication can feel like a hassle at times. But each dose is protecting your airways for the future and preventing a major attack."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If you're ever unsure whether you're using your inhaler correctly, or you'd like to try reducing your medication, please don't stop on your own — come talk to us anytime. Let's work side by side to protect your days of comfortable breathing."
        }
      ]
    }
