    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "title": "Please don't blame yourself for the label \"lifestyle disease\"",
      "excerpt": "This number is my own fault for not taking care of myself, isn't it — I want to tell the people who say this something important. Some cases of high blood pressure, diabetes, and dyslipidemia have nothing to do with lifestyle at all.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "When I explain checkup results, some patients apologize, saying something like, \"This number is my own fault for not taking care of myself, isn't it...\" Others look genuinely disheartened and ask, \"I'm exercising and watching my diet so carefully — why isn't it getting better?\" When people hear \"high blood pressure,\" \"diabetes,\" or \"dyslipidemia,\" the phrase \"lifestyle disease\" comes to mind first, and many end up feeling it's a result of their own undisciplined living."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Reading this may ease some of that self-blame. The short version: **not everyone diagnosed with high blood pressure, diabetes, or dyslipidemia actually has a lifestyle disease.** Here's why, with some concrete examples."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### The definition of \"lifestyle disease\" has exclusions"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "A lifestyle disease is defined as a condition whose onset and progression are influenced by habits such as diet, exercise, smoking, and drinking. What's less well known is that official guidance explicitly excludes familial (hereditary) conditions from this definition. In other words, even under the same diagnosis name, there are clearly cases caused by something other than lifestyle."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Common examples unrelated to lifestyle"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- **Type 1 diabetes**: caused by the body's immune system mistakenly attacking the insulin-producing cells in the pancreas, among other factors. It often develops in childhood or young adulthood and has nothing to do with diet or exercise.\n- **Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH)**: an inherited condition that causes very high cholesterol from a young age. No matter how carefully someone manages their diet, the numbers often won't come down fully, and early medication is usually necessary.\n- **Secondary and hereditary hypertension**: most high blood pressure is \"essential hypertension,\" where salt intake and obesity play a role, but a portion is \"secondary hypertension,\" caused by hormonal abnormalities, kidney disease, or certain medications. Some people also develop high blood pressure at a young age due to a strong family history."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Please don't carry that \"but I'm trying so hard\" alone"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "In clinic, I sometimes hear things like, \"Someone told me I must have eaten too many sweets to get diabetes, and it hurt,\" or \"I'm careful every day but my numbers won't budge, and it feels like I'm being blamed.\" It's genuinely painful to see people — or the people around them — assume, based on a diagnosis name alone, that it must be a failure of self-management."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "What matters is identifying what's actually behind an elevated number, and choosing the right approach for that person. For some, adjusting daily habits makes a real difference; for others, the best path forward is medication suited to their underlying condition."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If you've been flagged at a checkup but can't think of a reason why, or if you're working hard and the numbers still won't change, please don't carry that alone — feel free to come talk with us."
        }
      ]
    }
